L("returns", "Returns & risk", "Returns and compounding", 5,
  "Gains build on gains. Small differences in yearly return grow into large differences over decades.",
  [("Simple return", "Return is the change in value divided by where you started. Buy at $100, now worth $112: a 12% return. Losses work the same way: $100 to $85 is a −15% return."),
   ("Compounding", "When gains stay invested, next year's return applies to a bigger amount. $10,000 growing 7% a year becomes about $19,700 in 10 years and about $76,000 in 30 years. The last decade adds far more dollars than the first, even though the rate never changed."),
   ("Annualized return", "To compare periods of different lengths, returns are annualized: the steady yearly rate that would have produced the same total. A 50% total gain over 5 years is about 8.4% a year, not 10%, because of compounding. Signal Desk shows 3- and 5-year returns this way."),
   ("Losses need bigger gains", "A 50% loss needs a 100% gain to get back to even. A 20% loss needs 25%. This lopsidedness is why limiting deep losses matters so much.")],
  ["Return = change ÷ starting value.", "Compounding makes time your strongest tool.", "Annualized return lets you compare different periods.", "Big losses need much bigger gains to recover."],
  [("An investment falls 50%. What gain gets it back to where it started?", ["50%", "75%", "100%"], 2, "Half the money must double to recover: $50 → $100 is +100%."),
   ("A fund rose 50% over 5 years. Its annualized return is closest to:", ["10% a year", "8.4% a year", "12% a year"], 1, "1.5^(1/5) − 1 ≈ 8.4%. Compounding means you don't just divide by 5."),
   ("Why does the last decade of a 30-year investment add the most dollars?", ["Markets always rise faster later", "The same percentage applies to a much larger balance", "Fees disappear after 20 years"], 1, "That's compounding: the base keeps growing.")],
  ["Return", "Compounding", "Annualized return (CAGR)", "Total return"]),

L("risk", "Returns & risk", "Risk: volatility and drawdowns", 6,
  "Risk is how bumpy the ride is and how far you could fall. Higher expected returns usually come with more of both.",
  [("Volatility", "Volatility measures how much prices swing around their average, usually as the yearly standard deviation of returns. A broad stock fund often runs around 15–20% a year; a single tech stock may be 30–50%; bitcoin often 50% or more; a broad bond fund 5–7%. Higher volatility means a wider range of outcomes, good and bad."),
   ("Drawdown", "A drawdown is the fall from a peak to a later low. The maximum drawdown is the worst such fall in a period. It answers a gut-level question: how much would I have been down at the worst moment? Volatility and drawdown together describe the ride."),
   ("Beta", "Beta compares an investment's moves with the overall market (Signal Desk uses the S&P 500). A beta of 1 moves about in step; 1.5 tends to move 50% more in both directions; 0.5 about half as much. Beta says nothing about whether something will go up, only how it tends to move with the market."),
   ("Risk and reward", "Investors demand a higher expected return to hold something bumpier. That doesn't mean bumpier things always pay more; it means they have to be expected to, or no one would hold them. Risk you can't stomach is risk you'll sell at the bottom, which turns a temporary drop into a permanent loss.")],
  ["Volatility = how much prices swing.", "Max drawdown = the worst fall from a peak.", "Beta = how much it moves with the market.", "Only take risk you can sit through."],
  [("A stock has a beta of 1.5. If the market falls 10%, a typical move for the stock is:", ["Down about 15%", "Up about 15%", "Down about 1.5%"], 0, "Beta of 1.5 means roughly 1.5× the market's move, in the same direction."),
   ("What does maximum drawdown measure?", ["The average daily change", "The worst fall from a peak to a later low", "The difference between high and low on one day"], 1, "It's the deepest hole you'd have been in."),
   ("Which usually has the lowest volatility?", ["A single technology stock", "Bitcoin", "A broad bond fund"], 2, "Broad bond funds typically swing far less than stocks or crypto.")],
  ["Volatility", "Standard deviation", "Drawdown", "Maximum drawdown", "Beta", "Risk tolerance"]),

